Subject: give Qt a real WASI platform, mkspec and Platform module

WHAT
  cmake/QtPlatformSupport.cmake         defines WASI (deliberately NOT via
                                        qt_set01 -- see below) AND sets UNIX.
  cmake/QtMkspecHelpers.cmake           maps WASI -> the new mkspec.
  cmake/QtBuildRepoHelpers.cmake        adds WASI to the no-pkg-config list.
  mkspecs/common/wasi/qplatformdefs.h   NEW. mkspecs/common/wasm/'s file with
                                        everything wasi-libc lacks removed.
  mkspecs/common/wasi/wasi.conf         NEW.
  mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/{qmake.conf,qplatformdefs.h}  NEW.

WHY
  * CMake's own Platform/WASI.cmake is a comment and WASI-Initialize.cmake is a
    bare `set(WASI 1)` -- unlike Emscripten-Initialize.cmake, NEITHER sets
    UNIX. Qt gates its entire POSIX layer on `CONDITION UNIX`
    (src/corelib/CMakeLists.txt:702,720,736,844,854,893,1021,1051,1110,1193,
    1210 -- qcore_unix.cpp, qfilesystemengine_unix.cpp, qeventdispatcher_unix.
    cpp, qtimerinfo_unix.cpp, qlocale_unix.cpp). Without UNIX those sources are
    never added to the Core target, syncqt therefore never copies
    qcore_unix_p.h into the build include tree, and the build dies with
    "private/qcore_unix_p.h file not found" in a dozen files that have nothing
    obviously to do with it (qlogging.cpp, qrandom.cpp, qsysinfo.cpp,
    qfsfileengine.cpp, qtemporaryfile.cpp, qtranslator.cpp, ...).

    UNIX cannot be set from a toolchain file because CMake clears it
    afterwards. It also CANNOT be set the way Qt does it for Integrity, in
    cmake/platforms/Platform/WASI.cmake -- that was tried first and it silently
    does nothing. cmGlobalGenerator::EnableLanguage resolves
    `include(Platform/${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME})` against CMake's OWN Modules
    directory in preference to CMAKE_MODULE_PATH, which is precisely what the
    comment above the PREPEND in QtAutoDetectHelpers.cmake means by
    "CMake-provided platform modules take precedence". Integrity works only
    because CMake ships no Platform/Integrity.cmake; CMake 4.4.2 DOES ship
    Modules/Platform/WASI.cmake, so a Qt-side sibling is shadowed and never
    runs. Verified by probe: with ONLY qtbase/cmake/platforms on
    CMAKE_MODULE_PATH, a message() added to that file never prints and UNIX
    stays empty.

    Hence `set(UNIX 1)` lives in QtPlatformSupport.cmake, which is include()d
    from the macro qt_internal_setup_platform_support_variables() at top-level
    directory scope before add_subdirectory(src) -- so it reaches every module
    exactly the way WASM and ANDROID do.

  * WASI itself is set with a plain quoted `if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL
    "WASI")` and NOT with qt_set01, even though every other platform on that
    page uses qt_set01. This is a genuine trap, and it cost a whole build
    cycle. qt_set01 is `function(qt_set01 result) if(${ARGN}) ...`, and
    `if(${ARGN})` RE-EXPANDS its arguments unquoted; if() then dereferences any
    bare word that names a defined variable. CMake's own
    Platform/WASI-Initialize.cmake has ALREADY done `set(WASI 1)`, so
    `CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL WASI` degrades to `"WASI" STREQUAL "1"` and
    qt_set01 sets WASI to *0* on the one platform where it should be 1. Every
    other line gets away with it only because nothing defines a variable named
    `Linux`, `QNX` or `Android`. Reproduced in isolation with `cmake -P`:
    qt_set01 yields 0 where a directly quoted if() yields TRUE.
  * QtMkspecHelpers.cmake has no WASI arm, so QT_DEFAULT_MKSPEC stays empty and
    its `if(NOT QT_QMAKE_TARGET_MKSPEC OR NOT EXISTS ...)` FATAL_ERRORs before
    a single feature is evaluated. The directory is ALSO a real include path
    (QtPlatformTargetHelpers.cmake's qt_internal_get_platform_definition_
    include_dir), which is how Qt's `#include "qplatformdefs.h"` resolves --
    so it has to be a genuine header, not a stub.
  * mkspecs/common/wasm/qplatformdefs.h cannot be reused: it includes <grp.h>,
    <pwd.h>, <sys/ipc.h>, <sys/wait.h> and <net/if.h>, none of which exist in
    the wasi sysroot, and it defines QT_OPEN_LARGEFILE to O_LARGEFILE, which is
    an undeclared identifier on wasi-libc.
  * pkg-config would answer from the HOST's .pc files. WASM is already on that
    exclusion list for the same reason.

  Note WASI is deliberately a SEPARATE variable from WASM, not an alias. Qt's
  `AND NOT WASM` guards all mean "not emscripten" (embind, emscripten::val,
  EM_ASM, -s FETCH=1) and none of them are about wasm as a machine. Setting
  WASM=1 would pull in ~4,000 lines of embind code wasi-sdk cannot compile,
  inject emcc-only link flags onto the global Platform target, and EXCLUDE the
  minimal/offscreen QPA plugins that we want.

UPSTREAM
  not-as-is. The idea is right but upstream would want a whole Qt platform
  port -- CI, a QPA, docs, a supported-platform entry -- not four files. The
  qt_set01(WASI) and the pkg-config line are individually fine; the mkspec is
  the part that needs a real port around it. The qt_set01 collision, by
  contrast, is a plain latent bug in qt_set01 itself and IS worth reporting
  upstream: any platform whose CMake name matches an already-defined variable
  will be misdetected the same way. The `set(UNIX 1)` is arguably a bug report
  against CMake rather than against Qt:
  Platform/WASI-Initialize.cmake should set UNIX the way its Emscripten
  counterpart does, and if it ever did, this hunk could be dropped.

---
diff --git a/cmake/QtPlatformSupport.cmake b/cmake/QtPlatformSupport.cmake
index 9f8498e..bb2001f 100644
--- a/cmake/QtPlatformSupport.cmake
+++ b/cmake/QtPlatformSupport.cmake
@@ -20,6 +20,52 @@ qt_set01(FREEBSD CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "FreeBSD") # FIXME: How to identify
 qt_set01(NETBSD CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "NetBSD") # FIXME: How to identify this?
 qt_set01(WASM CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Emscripten" OR EMSCRIPTEN)
 qt_set01(WASM64 QT_QMAKE_TARGET_MKSPEC STREQUAL "wasm-emscripten-64")
+# WASI is wasm, but it is NOT Emscripten: no DOM, no embind, a POSIX libc
+# instead of a JS shim. It must therefore stay distinct from WASM above --
+# every `AND NOT WASM` guard in Qt is about emscripten-isms, not about wasm.
+#
+# NOT written as `qt_set01(WASI CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")`, even
+# though that is the form every line above uses. qt_set01 does `if(${ARGN})`,
+# which RE-EXPANDS its arguments unquoted, and if() dereferences a bare word
+# that names a defined variable. CMake's own Platform/WASI-Initialize.cmake has
+# already done `set(WASI 1)`, so the condition degrades to
+# `"WASI" STREQUAL "1"` and qt_set01 confidently sets WASI to 0. Every other
+# platform in this file gets away with it only because no variable is named
+# `Linux`, `QNX`, `Android`... Verified in isolation with `cmake -P`:
+# qt_set01 yields 0 where a directly quoted if() yields TRUE.
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    set(WASI 1)
+else()
+    set(WASI 0)
+endif()
+if(WASI)
+    # wasi-libc IS a POSIX libc, and Qt gates its entire POSIX layer on
+    # `CONDITION UNIX` -- qcore_unix.cpp, qfilesystemengine_unix.cpp,
+    # qfsfileengine_unix.cpp, qlockfile_unix.cpp, qtimerinfo_unix.cpp,
+    # qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp, qlocale_unix.cpp, qstandardpaths_unix.cpp.
+    # Without UNIX, QtCore compiles with no platform layer at all: the sources
+    # are never added, syncqt never copies qcore_unix_p.h, and the build dies
+    # on "private/qcore_unix_p.h file not found" in a dozen unrelated files.
+    #
+    # This must be done HERE and not in cmake/platforms/Platform/WASI.cmake the
+    # way Integrity does it. That idiom only works for a platform CMake does
+    # not already know: cmGlobalGenerator::EnableLanguage resolves
+    # `include(Platform/${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME})` against CMake's OWN Modules
+    # directory in preference to CMAKE_MODULE_PATH -- which is exactly what
+    # QtAutoDetectHelpers.cmake means by "CMake-provided platform modules take
+    # precedence". CMake ships Modules/Platform/WASI.cmake (a bare comment) and
+    # Modules/Platform/WASI-Initialize.cmake (`set(WASI 1)`, and notably NOT
+    # `set(UNIX 1)` the way Emscripten-Initialize.cmake does), so a Qt-side
+    # Platform/WASI.cmake is silently shadowed and never runs. Verified by
+    # probe: with ONLY qtbase/cmake/platforms on CMAKE_MODULE_PATH, a
+    # message() added to that file does not print and UNIX stays empty.
+    #
+    # QtPlatformSupport is include()d from the macro
+    # qt_internal_setup_platform_support_variables(), which runs at top-level
+    # directory scope before add_subdirectory(src), so this reaches every
+    # module the same way WASM and ANDROID do.
+    set(UNIX 1)
+endif()
 qt_set01(SOLARIS CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "SunOS")
 qt_set01(HURD CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "GNU")
 
diff --git a/cmake/QtMkspecHelpers.cmake b/cmake/QtMkspecHelpers.cmake
index a961296..ed3f852 100644
--- a/cmake/QtMkspecHelpers.cmake
+++ b/cmake/QtMkspecHelpers.cmake
@@ -60,6 +60,8 @@ macro(qt_internal_setup_platform_definitions_and_mkspec)
         set(QT_DEFAULT_MKSPEC macx-ios-clang)
     elseif(APPLE)
         set(QT_DEFAULT_MKSPEC macx-clang)
+    elseif(WASI)
+        set(QT_DEFAULT_MKSPEC wasi-clang-wasip2)
     elseif(WASM)
         if(WASM64)
             set(QT_DEFAULT_MKSPEC wasm-emscripten-64)
diff --git a/cmake/QtBuildRepoHelpers.cmake b/cmake/QtBuildRepoHelpers.cmake
index 57fc3da..0b56a59 100644
--- a/cmake/QtBuildRepoHelpers.cmake
+++ b/cmake/QtBuildRepoHelpers.cmake
@@ -1011,7 +1011,9 @@ function(qt_build_internals_disable_pkg_config_if_needed)
     set(pkg_config_enabled ON)
     qt_build_internals_find_pkg_config_executable()
 
-    if(APPLE OR WIN32 OR QNX OR ANDROID OR WASM OR (NOT PKG_CONFIG_EXECUTABLE))
+    # WASI joins this list for the same reason WASM is on it: there is no
+    # target pkg-config, so any .pc file found would describe the HOST.
+    if(APPLE OR WIN32 OR QNX OR ANDROID OR WASM OR WASI OR (NOT PKG_CONFIG_EXECUTABLE))
         set(pkg_config_enabled OFF)
     endif()
 
diff --git a/mkspecs/common/wasi/qplatformdefs.h b/mkspecs/common/wasi/qplatformdefs.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b192711
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mkspecs/common/wasi/qplatformdefs.h
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+// Copyright (C) 2024 The Qt Company Ltd.
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-Qt-Commercial OR LGPL-3.0-only OR GPL-2.0-only OR GPL-3.0-only
+
+#ifndef Q_WASI_PLATFORMDEFS_H
+#define Q_WASI_PLATFORMDEFS_H
+
+// Derived from mkspecs/common/wasm/qplatformdefs.h, with everything wasi-libc
+// does not have removed. The deletions, and why each one had to go:
+//
+//   <grp.h>, <pwd.h>   absent from the wasi sysroot entirely. There are no
+//                      users or groups in a wasm sandbox.
+//   <sys/ipc.h>        absent; no SysV IPC (FEATURE_sysv_shm/sem are OFF).
+//   <sys/wait.h>       absent; no fork/exec/wait (FEATURE_process is OFF).
+//   <net/if.h>         absent; wasip2 has no interface enumeration, so we
+//                      define QT_NO_IPV6IFNAME below rather than include it.
+//   <dlfcn.h>          present, but its dlopen is a stub over a static error
+//                      string. FEATURE_dlopen is OFF; not including it keeps
+//                      anything from being tempted.
+//
+// Everything else is byte-for-byte the wasm file, taking the
+// non-QT_LARGEFILE_SUPPORT arm of every macro pair: wasi-libc's off_t is
+// already 64-bit and none of the *64 symbols (stat64, lseek64, fopen64,
+// readdir64) exist.
+
+// Get Qt defines/settings
+
+#include "qglobal.h"
+
+// Set any POSIX/XOPEN defines at the top of this file to turn on specific APIs
+
+#include <unistd.h>
+
+// We are hot - unistd.h should have turned on the specific APIs we requested
+
+#include <features.h>
+#include <pthread.h>
+#include <dirent.h>
+#include <fcntl.h>
+#include <signal.h>
+
+#include <sys/types.h>
+#include <sys/ioctl.h>
+#include <sys/time.h>
+#include <sys/socket.h>
+#include <sys/stat.h>
+
+// wasip2 has no net/if.h and no if_nametoindex/if_indextoname. QHostAddress's
+// scope-id handling checks this macro and falls back to a plain string.
+#ifndef QT_NO_IPV6IFNAME
+#  define QT_NO_IPV6IFNAME
+#endif
+
+#ifndef _GNU_SOURCE
+#  define _GNU_SOURCE
+#endif
+
+// wasi-libc has no *64 variants; off_t is 64-bit natively.
+#define QT_STATBUF              struct stat
+#define QT_STATBUF4TSTAT        struct stat
+#define QT_STAT                 ::stat
+#define QT_FSTAT                ::fstat
+#define QT_LSTAT                ::lstat
+#define QT_OPEN                 ::open
+#define QT_TRUNCATE             ::truncate
+#define QT_FTRUNCATE            ::ftruncate
+#define QT_LSEEK                ::lseek
+
+#define QT_FOPEN                ::fopen
+#define QT_FSEEK                ::fseek
+#define QT_FTELL                ::ftell
+#define QT_FGETPOS              ::fgetpos
+#define QT_FSETPOS              ::fsetpos
+#define QT_MMAP                 ::mmap
+#define QT_FPOS_T               fpos_t
+#define QT_OFF_T                long
+
+#define QT_STAT_REG             S_IFREG
+#define QT_STAT_DIR             S_IFDIR
+#define QT_STAT_MASK            S_IFMT
+#define QT_STAT_LNK             S_IFLNK
+#define QT_SOCKET_CONNECT       ::connect
+#define QT_SOCKET_BIND          ::bind
+#define QT_FILENO               fileno
+#define QT_CLOSE                ::close
+#define QT_READ                 ::read
+#define QT_WRITE                ::write
+#define QT_ACCESS               ::access
+#define QT_GETCWD               ::getcwd
+#define QT_CHDIR                ::chdir
+#define QT_MKDIR                ::mkdir
+#define QT_RMDIR                ::rmdir
+// O_LARGEFILE is undeclared on wasi-libc ("use of undeclared identifier"); the
+// flag is meaningless here because off_t is already 64-bit.
+#define QT_OPEN_LARGEFILE       0
+#define QT_OPEN_RDONLY          O_RDONLY
+#define QT_OPEN_WRONLY          O_WRONLY
+#define QT_OPEN_RDWR            O_RDWR
+#define QT_OPEN_CREAT           O_CREAT
+#define QT_OPEN_TRUNC           O_TRUNC
+#define QT_OPEN_APPEND          O_APPEND
+#define QT_OPEN_EXCL            O_EXCL
+
+// Directory iteration
+#define QT_DIR                  DIR
+
+#define QT_OPENDIR              ::opendir
+#define QT_CLOSEDIR             ::closedir
+
+#define QT_DIRENT               struct dirent
+#define QT_READDIR              ::readdir
+
+#define QT_SIGNAL_RETTYPE       void
+#define QT_SIGNAL_ARGS          int
+#define QT_SIGNAL_IGNORE        SIG_IGN
+
+#define QT_SOCKLEN_T            socklen_t
+
+#if defined(_XOPEN_SOURCE) && (_XOPEN_SOURCE >= 500)
+#define QT_SNPRINTF             ::snprintf
+#define QT_VSNPRINTF            ::vsnprintf
+#endif
+
+#endif // Q_WASI_PLATFORMDEFS_H
diff --git a/mkspecs/common/wasi/wasi.conf b/mkspecs/common/wasi/wasi.conf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1c8064d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mkspecs/common/wasi/wasi.conf
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+#
+# qmake configuration for building with wasi-sdk clang, targeting wasm32-wasip2.
+#
+# The CMake build only needs this directory to EXIST and to carry a resolvable
+# qplatformdefs.h (QtMkspecHelpers.cmake FATAL_ERRORs on an unknown mkspec, and
+# QtPlatformTargetHelpers.cmake puts mkspecs/<spec> on the include path so that
+# Qt's `#include "qplatformdefs.h"` resolves). qmake itself is not used to build
+# this port. The file is kept honest anyway so `qmake -query` output is not a
+# lie, and so a future qmake-based consumer has something to start from.
+#
+# Deliberately NOT copied from ../wasm/wasm.conf: that file carries the whole
+# EMCC_COMMON_LFLAGS / -s ASYNCIFY / ASYNCIFY_IMPORTS block, which is emcc-only.
+#
+
+MAKEFILE_GENERATOR      = UNIX
+QMAKE_PLATFORM          = wasi wasm unix
+CONFIG                 += static no_plugin_manifest
+
+include(../gcc-base.conf)
+include(../clang.conf)
+
+QMAKE_CC                = $$(WASI_SDK)/bin/clang --target=wasm32-wasip2
+QMAKE_CXX               = $$(WASI_SDK)/bin/clang++ --target=wasm32-wasip2
+QMAKE_LINK              = $$QMAKE_CXX
+QMAKE_LINK_C            = $$QMAKE_CC
+QMAKE_AR                = $$(WASI_SDK)/bin/llvm-ar cqs
+QMAKE_RANLIB            = $$(WASI_SDK)/bin/llvm-ranlib
+QMAKE_NM                = $$(WASI_SDK)/bin/llvm-nm -P
+QMAKE_STRIP             =
+
+# wasm has no shared objects and no dlopen: everything is a static archive
+# linked into the final component.
+QMAKE_PREFIX_STATICLIB  = lib
+QMAKE_EXTENSION_STATICLIB = a
+QMAKE_PREFIX_SHLIB      =
+QMAKE_EXTENSION_SHLIB   =
+
+# See plugins/qt/wasip2.cmake for why this exact triple of flags: wasmtime
+# rejects wasi-sdk's default LEGACY exception encoding, and Qt's bundled
+# libpng/libjpeg need real setjmp.
+QMAKE_CFLAGS           += -D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL -D_WASI_EMULATED_MMAN -D_WASI_EMULATED_PROCESS_CLOCKS -D_WASI_EMULATED_GETPID
+QMAKE_CXXFLAGS         += $$QMAKE_CFLAGS
+QMAKE_CXXFLAGS         += -fwasm-exceptions -mllvm -wasm-use-legacy-eh=false
+QMAKE_LFLAGS           += -lunwind -lsetjmp -lwasi-emulated-signal -lwasi-emulated-mman -lwasi-emulated-process-clocks -lwasi-emulated-getpid
+
+load(qt_config)
diff --git a/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qmake.conf b/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qmake.conf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8bc4784
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qmake.conf
@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@
+# qmake configuration for building with wasi-sdk clang for wasm32-wasip2.
+#
+# Mirrors mkspecs/wasm-emscripten/qmake.conf: a one-line include of the shared
+# common/ config. See ../common/wasi/wasi.conf.
+
+include(../common/wasi/wasi.conf)
diff --git a/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qplatformdefs.h b/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qplatformdefs.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dbd849b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mkspecs/wasi-clang-wasip2/qplatformdefs.h
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+// Copyright (C) 2024 The Qt Company Ltd.
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-Qt-Commercial OR LGPL-3.0-only OR GPL-2.0-only OR GPL-3.0-only
+
+#ifndef QPLATFORMDEFS_H
+#define QPLATFORMDEFS_H
+
+#include "../common/wasi/qplatformdefs.h"
+
+#endif // QPLATFORMDEFS_H
